\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Where the class can change}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{A Bautin point is a Hopf point at which the first Lyapunov
coefficient vanishes, so the bifurcation changes between
subcritical and supercritical.
\citet{Theorem 8.3, p. 315}{kuznetsov2004elements} is the
topological normal form.
\citet{§6.3.5, p. 200}{izhikevich2007dynamical} writes the
same degeneracy: the cubic coefficient in the Hopf normal
form changes sign.}

\p{A Bogdanov–Takens point is an equilibrium whose Jacobian
has a double zero eigenvalue. The Hopf bifurcation and the
fold then occur in the same plane.
\citet{§6.3.3, p. 194}{izhikevich2007dynamical} distinguishes
this from a fold-Hopf point, which needs a third dimension.
\citet{§8.4, p. 316}{kuznetsov2004elements} reduces the
planar point to a normal form when two quadratic coefficients
are nonzero.}

\p{\citet{§7.3, p. 365}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} makes the
same assumption. A small change of a second parameter, such
as #{\phi}, moves the cell from one codimension-one
bifurcation to another.}
